<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Entitlements for the APP's own executable (Contents/MacOS/Unsloth) and
      nothing else. Entitlements are a property of a signed executable, not
      something a process passes to the children it spawns, so the bundled
      Python interpreter, node, llama-server and whisper-server carry their own
      set: see "Sign the bundled runtime and the macOS app" in
      .github/workflows/release-desktop.yml, which is what signs them.

      com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation is deliberately NOT here.
      It used to be, with the comment "Allow loading Python/venv libraries not
      signed by us", from when the Python stack was installed at first run into
      ~/.unsloth. That reasoning never applied to this executable: the Python
      stack runs in a separate process and its library loading is governed by
      that process's entitlements. This one is a Rust/Tauri binary that loads
      WebKit and other Apple frameworks plus its own statically linked code, and
      it dlopen()s nothing (no libloading, no pyo3, no externalBin sidecar, no
      Contents/Frameworks). Keeping the relief here would only mean the app's
      process could be made to load a library signed by anyone at all, which is
      the one process in the bundle where that matters most.
    -->
    <!--
      Spawning the Python backend and the install scripts needs no entitlement,
      but the app's own JIT-mapped pages do: WebKit's JavaScript engine is what
      needs this key, and every WKWebView host carries it.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Network access for backend health checks and HuggingFace API -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Permit microphone capture for local dictation -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
